Making use of the exponential transform, we define Jensen gaps for the convex and concave exposures as, respectively, JGX=∑i=14piexiX−e∑i=14pixiX,JGV=∑i=14piexiY−e∑i=14pixiV\begin{document}$\mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{X}}=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}}-e^{\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}}, \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{V}}=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{x_{i}^{\mathcal{Y}}}-e^{\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} x_{i}^{\mathcal{V}}}$\end{document}, which relate to statistical moments as follows. The moment generating function for the convex and concave exposures are given by, respectively: MX(t)=∑i=14pietxiX\begin{document}$M_{\mathcal{X}}(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{t x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}}$\end{document} and MV(t) = ∑i=14pietxiL\begin{document}$\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{t x_{i}^{\mathcal{L}}}$\end{document}. The series expansion of the exponential function allows us to derive:
MX(1)=1+m1X+12!m2X+13!m3X+⋯+1N!mNX+…MV(1)=1+m1V+12!m2V+13!m3V+⋯+1N!mNV+… \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & M_{\mathcal{X}}(1)=1+m_{1}^{\mathcal{X}}+\frac{1}{2!} m_{2}^{\mathcal{X}}+\frac{1}{3!} m_{3}^{\mathcal{X}}+\cdots+\frac{1}{N!} m_{N}^{\mathcal{X}}+\ldots \\ & M_{\mathcal{V}}(1)=1+m_{1}^{\mathcal{V}}+\frac{1}{2!} m_{2}^{\mathcal{V}}+\frac{1}{3!} m_{3}^{\mathcal{V}}+\cdots+\frac{1}{N!} m_{N}^{\mathcal{V}}+\ldots\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where mXN and mVN are the N -th statistical raw moments of the convex and concave exposures, respectively. For example, mNX=∑i=14pi(xiX)N\begin{document}$m_{N}^{\mathcal{X}}=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i}\left(x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}\right)^{N}$\end{document} for any integer N ≥ 1. Jensen’s gaps for both exposures are then given by JGX=MX(1)−em1X,JGV=MV(1)−em\begin{document}$\mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{X}}=M_{\mathcal{X}}(1)-e^{m_{1}^{\mathcal{X}}}, \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{V}}=M_{\mathcal{V}}(1)-e^{m}$\end{document}. Using again the series expansion of the exponential of the first moment for each exposure, it follows that both positive Jensen gaps can be written in terms of moments, as follows:
(3)
JGX=∑k=2∞mkX−(m1X)kk!,JGV=∑k=2∞mkV−(m1V)kk! \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{X}}=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{m_{k}^{\mathcal{X}}-\left(m_{1}^{\mathcal{X}}\right)^{k}}{k!}, \quad \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{V}}=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{m_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}-\left(m_{1}^{\mathcal{V}}\right)^{k}}{k!} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Note that the sums in equation (3) nicely allow for a straightforward decomposition in terms on all raw moments with order larger than two.

As a robustness check, we introduce in our augmented Q-learning models the range principle due to [36] - see also [34] - which captures the notion that subjective evaluation of rewards may take into account their range through the Min-Max normalization presented below. That is, we replace objective rewards by their subjective judgements s(.) as follows:


s(rk)=rk−rminkrmaxk−rminkwithk∈{g,l}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle s(r^k) = \frac{r^k - r^k_\mathrm{min}}{r^k_\mathrm{max} - r^k_\mathrm{min}} \quad \mathrm{with} \quad k \in\{g, l\}$$\end{document}



Subjective judgements were then substituted in equation (1) for gains and losses and the resulting Q-learning models were estimated using the procedure described in Material and Methods, section Augmented Q-Learning Model Estimation and Simulation. More precisely, subjective judgements for gains and losses are as follows: s(rg) = (rg − 1)/(80 − 1) for options that give convex gains and s(rg) = (rg− 2)/(5 − 2) for options that give concave gains; similarly, s(rl) = (rl+ 15)/(−6 + 15) when losses are convex losses and s(rl) = (rl + 240)/(−3 + 240) when losses are concave. REE are then used (as Min and Max values) to normalize all rewards and they are included in the Q-sub-values when they happen. This implies, by definition, that subjective judgements equal zero when Black Swans materialize, and equal one when Jackpots occur.
